issue_context.body:
## 🚀 Feature Proposal

In some cases, it might be useful for our users to know if they are inside a 404 handling or not. We can quickly check it internally via the `kFourOhFourContext` symbol, but I think it would be great to expose it in our public API via the request object.

## Motivation

A use case example can be found [here](https://twitter.com/ThisIsMissEm/status/1240644657970806791).
TL;DR, you may not want to parse the body in case of a 404 request and close the request as soon as possible.

## Example

```js
fastify.addHook('onRequest', (req, reply, done) => {
  if (req.is404) {
    // specific handling
  }
  done()
})
```

The `is404` should always be present in the request object defaulting to `false`, and should be set to `true` automatically by Fastify.

comments:
@ThisIsMissEm yes, it looks like so!
I think the best approach would be to expose the `is404` in a nice way and let you handle the issue, or even build a plugin.
I would avoid adding yet another option to the framework itself as we should try to keep them at the minimum and suggest to use a plugin instead :)

@Eomm that could work, but I fear it is not robust enough. As soon as someone (you or an hook) changes the status code you no longer know that you were handling a 404.

I think we should provide a reliable way to tell the users that they are inside a 404 handling.
In this way also external plugins will be more robust, as we are guaranteeing if it is a 404 handling or not.
